// THE PROTECTED PUBLISHER (gunbc#10958 Phase B), AND EVERY STRUCTURAL REASON IT IS SAFE TO GIVE IT A
// CREDENTIAL.
//
// IT RUNS FROM THE DEFAULT BRANCH. A workflow_run workflow is taken from the default branch's own
// definition, never from the pull request's, so nothing a branch can edit decides what this job does.
//
// IT NEVER FETCHES THE PULL REQUEST'S TREE. Its checkout takes no ref, so it is this workflow's own
// commit; the branch's bytes arrive only as a downloaded candidate that the publisher reads as data
// and re-derives every fact about. `publisher_job_executes_candidate_code` is the emit-time check.
//
// IT RUNS ON A GITHUB-HOSTED RUNNER, and that is a credential-isolation fact rather than a capacity
// choice: our self-hosted runners also execute pull-request jobs and share a filesystem between
// slots, so a token minted on one of them is reachable from code a pull request wrote. The federation
// condition pins runner_environment to github-hosted for the same reason, so a later edit that moved
// this job onto the fleet would fail to federate rather than quietly widen.
//
// ADMISSION HAPPENS BEFORE FEDERATION, AND WITHOUT ANY CREDENTIAL AT ALL. The admit step is emitted with
// no GITHUB_TOKEN and no publication credential: observe_candidate_world_wet answers head liveness with
// a credential-free ls-remote of refs/pull/<n>/head and the base tree with a credential-free fetch into an
// isolated store, so it reaches no auth-declaring operation. A candidate REFUSED AT ADMIT (malformed, with a
// moved head, for the wrong repository, or sealed against a non-default base) fails the admit step, so the
// downstream publish step (gated on the admit step success) never runs, and no credential of any kind
// is ever requested for it. What admit does NOT do is read the pull request open/closed state or its identity --
// no credential-free transport answers those -- so a CLOSED or RETARGETED pull request still passes admit. The
// The order after admit lives in the seed, not here (gunbc.design_argument::single_authority: one place states an effect order -- see
// heal_publisher_cloud_credential and heal_publication_publish_for): the publish step re-admits, checks the
// pull request open and identical under the job GITHUB_TOKEN, and ONLY THEN mints the cloud credential
// (assertion, STS exchange, impersonation) and the App INSTALLATION token, then writes, reads back and
// receipts. The check therefore sits BEFORE every short-lived credential, so a closed or retargeted pull
// request costs no token at all -- which is a stronger property than the step order this comment used to
// describe, where a closed PR still paid a federation exchange before the check.

// THE PUBLISHER'S NAME, JOB ID AND TIMEOUT COME FROM THE REPOSITORY'S CI PROFILE ROW. Two of these
// literals used to sit beside the step list while the profile also described the job; the row is now the
// single description and this emitter is a projection of it. The committed bytes are compared against this
// emission by the authority that owns generated artifacts (dag/gunbc/instruments/tier_o_drift_check.dag),
// which is the check the drift-watch job already runs; a claim re-quoting this file would be a second
// description of the same bytes.
data heal_publisher_no_job_refusal_reason: String = "REFUSED gunbc.heal_publisher_workflow: the repository's CI profile row declares no publisher (gunbc.ci.repository_ci_profile RepositoryCiProfile publisher is Absent). A publisher whose candidate source is not declared is a job that fires with nothing to publish - the shape this repository must not be able to commit. Delete .github/workflows/heal-publish.yml, or declare the heal or drift job that seals the candidate."

// THE REFUSAL FOR THE CASE THE PUBLISHER HAS NO CONSUMER FOR. Minted here, in the module that owns the
// publisher's reasons, rather than in the projection: the projection says only that the case is not one this
// emitter can be built from, and this is the module that knows why (its two phases would run a compiler the
// row's build steps never produced).
data heal_publisher_seed_route_without_a_composition_refusal_reason: String = "REFUSED gunbc.heal_publisher_workflow: the row's CompilerSource is Declared_seed -- it builds a second compiler from a declared seed but names no overlay to compose, so there is no root the publisher's phases could run that compiler in. Admit and publish would run this checkout's own `target/release/gunbc`, a binary these build steps never produced: a candidate sealed from one compiler and admitted against another, which is the drift this workflow exists to prevent (gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed, no fabricated default). A publisher that runs under a seed it built declares Declared_seed_under_composed_overlay; a publisher that runs this checkout's floor build declares Own_checkout_under_witness_floor. A lane that runs its roster under a seed compiler it built has a consumer and is emitted by gunbc.witness.repository_witness_workflow, not by this emitter."

data heal_publisher_candidate_directory: String = "heal-candidate"

data heal_publisher_build_step_name: String = "Build the publisher this job runs from the default branch"

// THE STEP NAME THE SEED ROUTE INSTALLS THE TOOLCHAIN UNDER. Step display names are this emitter's to
// own, as the build and publish names above are; the seed route and the toolchain route differ in which
// steps they run, not in who names them.
data heal_publisher_toolchain_install_step_name: String = "Install Rust toolchain"

// GitHub's workflow_run branch filter takes the TRIGGERING run's branch, and this publisher answers
// for every pull request branch, so the filter is the match-all pattern rather than an omission --
// the trigger requires the member, and `[]` would name no branch at all.
data heal_publisher_triggering_branches: List<String> = ["**"]

// ONE PUBLICATION AT A TIME PER BRANCH, AND NONE DROPPED. Two candidates for one branch are ordered
// rather than raced: the second re-reads the branch head after the first has moved it and refuses as
// stale, which is a decision, while a cancelled pending entry would be a repair silently lost.
data heal_publisher_concurrency: ConcurrencySpec = ConcurrencyMappingQueueMax {
  group: "heal-publish-${{ github.event.workflow_run.head_branch }}",
  cancel_in_progress: Present { value: QueueMaxCancelInProgressFalse }
}

fn heal_publisher_job_condition() -> String {
  serialize_expression(expression: LogicalAnd {
    left: Equals {
      left: ContextAccess { context: Github, path: ["event", "workflow_run", "event"] },
      right: StringLiteral { value: "pull_request" }
    },
    right: Equals {
      left: ContextAccess { context: Github, path: ["event", "workflow_run", "head_repository", "full_name"] },
      right: ContextAccess { context: Github, path: ["repository"] }
    }
  })
}

fn heal_publisher_checkout_step() -> Step {
  UsesStep {
    name: Present { value: "Checkout the default branch this publisher runs from" },
    id: none,
    uses: checkout_action,
    with: Present {
      value: [
        kv(key: "fetch-depth", value: yaml_int(n: 1)),
        kv(key: "persist-credentials", value: yaml_bool(b: false))
      ]
    },
    env: none,
    if_condition: none,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// A PATTERN RATHER THAN A NAME, because a name that matches nothing is an ERROR in the download
// action and "nothing to publish" is this workflow's ordinary case. The run id is the triggering
// run's, so the artifact read is bound to the run whose facts the publisher validates against.
// ABSENCE AND FAILURE ARE STILL DIFFERENT ANSWERS, AND THE ACTION ALREADY KEEPS THEM APART. With a
// pattern (no name) the action treats "matched nothing" as zero artifacts downloaded and a transient
// or unauthorized download as a failure, so this step carries no probe and no continue-on-error: a
// run that sealed nothing leaves the candidate directory empty, every later step gates on the blob
// files being present, and a failed download is a failed job.
fn heal_publisher_download_step() -> Step {
  UsesStep {
    name: Present { value: "Download the candidate the triggering heal run produced" },
    id: none,
    uses: download_artifact_action,
    with: Present {
      value: [
        kv(key: "pattern", value: yaml_string(s: heal_repair_candidate_artifact_name as String)),
        kv(key: "path", value: yaml_string(s: heal_publisher_candidate_directory)),
        kv(key: "merge-multiple", value: yaml_bool(b: true)),
        kv(key: "run-id", value: yaml_string(s: "${{ github.event.workflow_run.id }}")),
        kv(key: "github-token", value: yaml_string(s: "${{ github.token }}"))
      ]
    },
    env: none,
    if_condition: none,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// A CANDIDATE THAT NAMES PATHS HAS BLOBS BESIDE ITS MANIFEST, AND THAT IS WHAT THE CREDENTIALED
// STEPS GATE ON. The manifest itself is written on EVERY successful heal run -- an empty entries list
// is how a clean tree says so -- which is exactly why its presence cannot gate anything: it is there
// either way. The blob files are written only for paths that actually drifted, so their presence is
// the candidate's own statement that there is something to publish.
//
// WHAT THIS BUYS IS NOT A SAVED MINUTE. Gating the mint means a token for the publisher identity, and
// an installation token for the App, come into existence only on runs where a repair is owed. Every
// other run of this job holds no publication credential at any moment, which is the same property the
// candidate phase has by construction -- so the credentialed window is as narrow as the repair
// population rather than as wide as the pull-request population.
fn heal_publisher_candidate_has_entries_expression() -> Expression {
  NotEquals {
    left: FunctionCall {
      function: HashFiles,
      args: [StringLiteral { value: join([heal_publisher_candidate_directory, "/", heal_repair_candidate_blob_file_prefix as String, "*"], "") }]
    },
    right: StringLiteral { value: "" }
  }
}

fn heal_publisher_candidate_has_entries_condition() -> String {
  serialize_expression(expression: heal_publisher_candidate_has_entries_expression())
}

fn heal_publisher_gated(step_name: String, run: String, env: List<YamlKeyValue>?, id: String?, if_condition: String?) -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: step_name },
    id: id,
    run: run,
    shell: none,
    env: env,
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: if_condition,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// THE CANDIDATE DIRECTORY IS ONE NAME WITH TWO RENDERINGS, because its consumers resolve differently and
// getting this wrong is silent. The download step and the `hashFiles` gate run at the job workspace and stay
// RELATIVE there - an absolute path makes `hashFiles` match nothing, which would quietly retire the gate.
// A COMPOSED phase step is different: it cds into the composed root under `runner.temp` before it runs, so a
// relative value would resolve inside the composed tree, where the downloaded candidate has never been - the
// phases would report "no candidates" for a candidate sitting in the workspace, the same quiet-green shape
// as a lost artifact. So the composed route carries the SAME directory anchored to the workspace, which
// resolves identically from wherever its shell stands; the own route keeps the workspace-relative spelling
// because it never leaves the workspace, and both name one constant.
fn heal_publisher_candidate_directory_absolute() -> String {
  join(["${{ github.workspace }}/", heal_publisher_candidate_directory], "")
}

// COMPOSED IS A PROJECTION OF THE COMPILER SOURCE, not a second fact: the phases compose the root exactly when
// the compiler they run was built into that root. Deriving it from the same value the route steps were built
// from is what keeps "the build produced X" and "the phases ran X" from drifting apart.
fn heal_publisher_candidate_is_composed(source: PublisherCompilerSource) -> Bool {
  match source {
    PublisherRunsUnderComposedSeed { seed: _, overlay: _ } => true,
    PublisherBuildsItsOwnCheckout { required_bins: _ } => false
  }
}

fn heal_publisher_candidate_directory_env_row(composed: Bool) -> YamlKeyValue {
  let spelling = if composed {
    heal_publisher_candidate_directory_absolute()
  } else {
    heal_publisher_candidate_directory
  }
  kv(key: heal_publication_candidate_directory_env as String, value: yaml_string(s: spelling))
}

// THE CREDENTIAL-FREE GATE. It reads the event, the candidate and the forge with the job's own
// read-only token, and refuses before anything can be minted. It runs only when the candidate names
// paths: since gunbc#11791 the question "did the triggering run seal a candidate at all" is the
// download step's, which separates absence from a failed read,
// so a clean tree and a lost candidate no longer share one quiet green here.
// WHICH SUBJECT THE GATE RUNS, AND OVER WHICH ROOTS, ARE THE ROW'S: the invocation is the ci_spec target
// the profile carries, and a mirror's publisher runs it over the composed tree its drift job sealed
// against rather than over its own checkout - the same compose pieces the drift emitter reads, from the
// same overlay row, because a candidate admitted against a tree other than the one it was sealed from is
// the drift this workflow exists to prevent.
fn heal_publisher_admit_step(profile: RepositoryCiProfile, job: HealPublisherPolicy, source: PublisherCompilerSource) -> Outcome<Step> {
  match heal_publisher_phase_script(profile: profile, phase: job.admit_invocation, source: source) {
    Accepted { value: script, diagnostics: _ } =>
      outcome_accepted(
        value: heal_publisher_gated(
          step_name: heal_publisher_admit_step_name(profile: profile),
          run: script,
          env: Present {
            value: [
              heal_publisher_candidate_directory_env_row(composed: heal_publisher_candidate_is_composed(source: source))
            ]
          },
          id: none,
          if_condition: Present { value: heal_publisher_candidate_has_entries_condition() }
        )
      )
    Rejected { diagnostics: d } => Rejected { diagnostics: d }
  }
}

// THE GATE SAYS WHAT IT ADMITS AGAINST, and the wording depends on the route: a publisher over its own
// checkout admits against this checkout's policy; a publisher that copied an overlay into a detached tree
// admits against this checkout plus that overlay. One string cannot be truthful for both.
fn heal_publisher_admit_step_name(profile: RepositoryCiProfile) -> String {
  match profile_overlay(profile: profile) {
    Absent => "Admit the candidate against this checkout's policy, with no publication credential"
    Present { value: _ } =>
      "Admit the candidate against the composed policy (this checkout plus the overlay), with no publication credential"
  }
}

// ONE RENDER FOR BOTH PUBLISHER PHASES. Over its own checkout a phase is a `gunbc run` inside the memory
// ceiling; over a composed root it is that same ceiling, then the composition, then the subject run through
// the overlay prefix. The ceiling, the composition and the launcher are all imported - ci_spec's prelude,
// the drift emitter's compose pieces, the same `--source-root` word list - so nothing here re-spells a
// command line, and the roots come from the row rather than from a constant in this module.
// WHAT EACH PHASE ACTUALLY RUNS, decided by the SAME value that decided the build. Dispatching on whether a
// composition happens to exist was the defect: a row that built a second compiler but had no overlay to compose
// fell into the own-checkout launcher and ran `target/release/gunbc` -- a binary its build steps never produced,
// in a checkout the profile never named. The sum says which compiler the row's build produced, and the phases
// run THAT one: this checkout's floor build under `PublisherBuildsItsOwnCheckout`, the seed's own binary under
// `PublisherRunsUnderComposedSeed` (composed through `compose_gunbc_run_statements`, which cds into the
// composed root and runs the seed compiler it stands there).
fn heal_publisher_phase_script(
  profile: RepositoryCiProfile,
  phase: DagInvocation,
  source: PublisherCompilerSource
) -> Outcome<String> {
  match source {
    PublisherBuildsItsOwnCheckout { required_bins: _ } =>
      outcome_accepted(
        value: gunbc_run_step_script_with_prelude(
          prelude: heal_publisher_cgroup_prelude(),
          source_roots: profile.roster.source_roots,
          entry: phase.entry as String,
          function: phase.function as String,
          claim_run: false,
          receipt_rel: none
        )
      )
    PublisherRunsUnderComposedSeed { seed: seed, overlay: overlay } =>
      bind_outcome(
        o: bash_emit_stmts(
          statements: concat(
            compose_preamble_statements(
              seed: seed,
              c: merge_checkout_compose(
                seed: seed,
                overlay: overlay,
                notes: [overlay_note(overlay: overlay), local_runs_note(overlay: overlay)]
              )
            ),
            compose_gunbc_run_statements(
              overlay: overlay,
              source_roots: composed_source_roots(profile_roots: profile.roster.source_roots, overlay: overlay),
              entry: phase.entry,
              function: phase.function
            )
          ),
          set_e: true
        ),
        f: fn(tail) { outcome_accepted(value: concat(heal_publisher_cgroup_prelude_script(), tail)) }
      )
  }
}

// THE PUBLICATION PHASE, over the same roots the gate admitted: the row's publish invocation, rendered by
// the one phase-script function. It carries the job token for the openness check and NOTHING ELSE: no
// Google credential and no installation token enters this step's environment. The cloud credential is
// minted inside the subject (heal_publication heal_publisher_cloud_credential) one call above the read
// that spends it, and the installation token inside mint_publisher_installation_token. Neither is ever
// rendered into shell here, which is what keeps both out of every child process this step spawns.
fn heal_publisher_publish_step(profile: RepositoryCiProfile, job: HealPublisherPolicy, source: PublisherCompilerSource) -> Outcome<Step> {
  match heal_publisher_phase_script(profile: profile, phase: job.publish_invocation, source: source) {
    Accepted { value: script, diagnostics: _ } =>
      outcome_accepted(
        value: heal_publisher_gated(
          step_name: "Publish the admitted repair, fast-forward only, and read it back",
          run: script,
          env: Present {
            value: [
              heal_publisher_candidate_directory_env_row(composed: heal_publisher_candidate_is_composed(source: source)),
              kv(key: "GITHUB_TOKEN", value: yaml_string(s: "${{ github.token }}"))
            ]
          },
          id: none,
          if_condition: Present { value: heal_publisher_candidate_has_entries_condition() }
        )
      )
    Rejected { diagnostics: d } => Rejected { diagnostics: d }
  }
}

// THE PUBLISHER'S TOOLCHAIN AND BUILD RUN ONLY WHEN A REPAIR IS OWED (operator, 2026-09-29). The
// binary they produce has exactly two consumers, the admit and publish steps, and both are gated on
// heal_publisher_candidate_has_entries_condition. Building it unconditionally spent ~5.5 minutes of
// a GitHub-hosted runner on every pull request's witnesses completion to produce a binary nothing
// then ran -- the ordinary case, since most runs seal an empty candidate -- and in a hosted pool of
// 20 that cost is what queued real repairs for hours. The gate is the consumers' own condition, so
// the build cannot be skipped on a run whose candidate a consumer would read.
fn heal_publisher_only_when_repair_owed(step: Step) -> Step {
  let owed = heal_publisher_candidate_has_entries_expression()
  match step {
    RunStep { name: n, id: i, run: r, shell: sh, env: e, working_directory: w, if_condition: c, continue_on_error: co, timeout_minutes: t } =>
      RunStep { name: n, id: i, run: r, shell: sh, env: e, working_directory: w, if_condition: Present { value: heal_publisher_condition_and(existing: c, owed: owed) }, continue_on_error: co, timeout_minutes: t }
    UsesStep { name: n, id: i, uses: u, with: wi, env: e, if_condition: c, continue_on_error: co, timeout_minutes: t } =>
      UsesStep { name: n, id: i, uses: u, with: wi, env: e, if_condition: Present { value: heal_publisher_condition_and(existing: c, owed: owed) }, continue_on_error: co, timeout_minutes: t }
  }
}

fn heal_publisher_condition_and(existing: String?, owed: Expression) -> String {
  match existing {
    Present { value: _ } => serialize_expression(expression: Equals { left: StringLiteral { value: "wrapped step carries its own condition" }, right: StringLiteral { value: "" } })
    Absent => serialize_expression(expression: owed)
  }
}

type HealPublisherBoundStep {
  step: Step
  role: StepCapabilityRole
}

// THE STEPS THAT BUILD THE COMPILER THE PUBLISHER'S PHASES RUN UNDER, selected by the row's
// `CompilerSource` and by nothing else - never by which other fields happen to be present, which is how this
// module once gave one repository another repository's build. Each case carries the facts its own build
// needs: the own-checkout case names the binaries to build from this checkout under the witness floor's
// toolchain, and both seed cases hand their declared seed to the one seed build below. A case cannot be
// handed a row that lacks what it builds from, so there is no arm here that invents a step to fill a gap.
fn heal_publisher_route_steps(source: PublisherCompilerSource) -> Outcome<List<HealPublisherBoundStep>> {
  match source {
    PublisherBuildsItsOwnCheckout { required_bins: bins } =>
      outcome_accepted(
        value: [
          HealPublisherBoundStep {
            step: heal_publisher_only_when_repair_owed(step: toolchain_home_isolation_step()),
            role: capability_neutral,
          },
          HealPublisherBoundStep {
            step: heal_publisher_only_when_repair_owed(step: witness_floor_toolchain_step()),
            role: provides_only(capabilities: [CargoCapability, RustcCapability, RustfmtCapability, RustupCapability]),
          },
          HealPublisherBoundStep {
            step: heal_publisher_only_when_repair_owed(step: toolchain_pin_rustup_default_step()),
            role: consumes_only(capabilities: [RustupCapability, CargoCapability]),
          },
          HealPublisherBoundStep {
            step: heal_publisher_only_when_repair_owed(
              step: witness_floor_build_step(
                step_name: heal_publisher_build_step_name,
                build_script: repo_self_build_command(bins: list_map(xs: bins, f: fn(b) { b as String }))
              )
            ),
            role: consumes_only(capabilities: [CargoCapability]),
          },
        ]
      )
    PublisherRunsUnderComposedSeed { seed: seed, overlay: _ } => heal_publisher_seed_route_steps(seed: seed)
  }
}

// THE SEED BUILD, ONE IMPLEMENTATION FOR BOTH SEED VARIANTS: the checkout, the toolchain install and the
// seed's own build command, gated on a repair being owed -- the same steps the drift emitter runs before it
// seals, imported rather than re-spelled so the compiler that built the candidate and the compiler the
// publisher runs cannot come from different recipes. NO STEP IS INVENTED WHEN A FOLD FAILS: the seed
// grammar's own refusal travels outward, and a caller that reconstructed a cause from a field would report a
// failure that did not happen.
// THE ROLE OF A TOOLCHAIN-LANE STEP, DERIVED FROM THE STEP AND NOT FROM WHERE IT SITS IN A LIST.
// gunbc.toolchain_home_standing is already the authority on what an install IS (a UsesStep of the upstream
// setup action) and what an isolation IS (the run step that wipes and rebinds HOME, CARGO_HOME and
// RUSTUP_HOME under RUNNER_TEMP); the pin is asked of the same module, by the command rows that make it
// the pin. Asking IT those questions here means the capability role and the standing verdict cannot
// disagree about the same step -- and a role read off a list position would be a second, hand-kept
// account of the same fact, which is the shape that let the seed arm install into a shared home while
// its own-checkout sibling composed the pair.
//
// THERE IS NO FALL-THROUGH ARM. A final `else` labelling whatever was left the default-toolchain pin
// would be a fabricated default (gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed): any step at all -- a checkout, an upload, a build step --
// would receive the pin's consumes-only by being unnamed, and the classification would stop deciding
// anything about the step it annotated. A lane member the module cannot recognise is a lane that lost
// that member, and it refuses with a reason rather than being assigned a role.
fn heal_publisher_seed_toolchain_role(step: Step) -> Outcome<StepCapabilityRole> {
  if step_installs_toolchain(step: step) {
    outcome_accepted(value: provides_only(capabilities: [CargoCapability, RustcCapability, RustfmtCapability, RustupCapability]))
  } else if step_isolates_toolchain_homes(step: step) {
    outcome_accepted(value: capability_neutral)
  } else if step_pins_default_toolchain(step: step) {
    outcome_accepted(value: consumes_only(capabilities: [RustupCapability, CargoCapability]))
  } else {
    outcome_rejected(
      d: module_unavailable_diagnostic(
        reason: "heal_publisher_seed_lane_step_unrecognised: the seed toolchain lane is the isolation, the install and the pin, each recognised by gunbc.toolchain_home_standing's own checks, and this step is none of them",
        module_file: "gunbc.heal_publisher_workflow"
      )
    )
  }
}

// THE FOLD'S SEED, TYPED. An empty list literal in an argument position has no expected collection to
// read its element type from, so the element type is stated once, in the return type of the helper that
// hands it over.
fn heal_publisher_seed_bound_steps_empty() -> List<HealPublisherBoundStep> {
  []
}

// THE LANE ENTERS THE ROUTE AS A UNIT, AND THE UNIT IS AS SOUND AS ITS MEMBERS. Each member's role is
// asked of the step itself, above; the first member the module cannot recognise refuses the whole route,
// so a lane that lost a member never reaches the emitted YAML -- where the whole-workflow admission from
// gunbc.toolchain_home_standing would refuse the file anyway. Refusing at emission keeps the reason
// beside the fact that broke.
//
// WHY THE ORDER LIVES IN gunbc.ci.repository_seed_steps AND NOT HERE. THE LANE CARRIES ITS OWN ORDER
// (isolation before install before pin): the isolation wipes and re-points HOME/CARGO_HOME/RUSTUP_HOME
// under a per-job RUNNER_TEMP directory, and a self-hosted runner -- which is exactly where the seed
// arm runs, in a declared fleet group -- keeps its home between jobs, so an install placed first lands
// in a toolchain home shared with every other job on the machine and the pin then selects from it. The
// standing module refuses that file by name, and it refused THIS job by name before the fix. The route
// splices the shared lane's steps and owns only what a publisher owes a step, which is its role.
fn heal_publisher_seed_lane_bound_steps() -> Outcome<List<HealPublisherBoundStep>> {
  fold(
    xs: seed_toolchain_lane_steps(lane: seed_toolchain_lane(step_name: heal_publisher_toolchain_install_step_name)),
    init: outcome_accepted(value: heal_publisher_seed_bound_steps_empty()),
    f: fn(acc, step) {
      match acc {
        Rejected { diagnostics: d } => Rejected { diagnostics: d }
        Accepted { value: bound_so_far, diagnostics: _ } =>
          match heal_publisher_seed_toolchain_role(step: step) {
            Rejected { diagnostics: d } => Rejected { diagnostics: d }
            Accepted { value: role, diagnostics: _ } =>
              outcome_accepted(
                value: concat(
                  bound_so_far,
                  [HealPublisherBoundStep {
                    step: heal_publisher_only_when_repair_owed(step: step),
                    role: role,
                  }]
                )
              )
          }
      }
    }
  )
}

fn heal_publisher_seed_route_steps(seed: PublicSeed) -> Outcome<List<HealPublisherBoundStep>> {
  // The pin is read from the ROW here, not passed in beside it: the guard below compares what the
  // verifier answered against the same value the row declares, and a pin threaded in from a caller would
  // be a second authority over which commit the publisher is allowed to build.
  match seed_source_pin(source: seed.source) {
    Absent {  } => outcome_rejected(
      d: module_unavailable_diagnostic(
        reason: seed_source_refusal_reason(source: seed.source),
        module_file: "gunbc.heal_publisher_workflow"
      )
    )
    Present { value: pin } =>
  match seed_build_step(seed: seed) {
    Rejected { diagnostics: d } => Rejected { diagnostics: d }
    Accepted { value: build_step, diagnostics: _ } =>
      match seed_lane_steps(seed: seed, pin: pin) {
        Rejected { diagnostics: d } => Rejected { diagnostics: d }
        Accepted { value: seed_lane, diagnostics: _ } =>
          // THE TOOLCHAIN LANE AS ONE UNIT -- isolation, install, pin, in the order
          // gunbc.ci.repository_seed_steps owns, each member's role asked of the step itself. This arm
          // publishes from a self-hosted fleet group, so the install lands on a runner whose home
          // persists and is shared with every other slot: the unisolated install this composition makes
          // unconstructible is the one gunbc.toolchain_home_standing refuses, and it refused THIS job by
          // name before the fix. An unrecognisable member refuses the route here.
          match heal_publisher_seed_lane_bound_steps() {
            Rejected { diagnostics: d } => Rejected { diagnostics: d }
            Accepted { value: lane_bound, diagnostics: _ } =>
              outcome_accepted(
                value: concat(
                  [
                    // THE GUARD BEFORE THE CHECKOUT, gated on the same condition the checkout is: a lane
                    // that checks out the seed at all is a lane that has to have been told which commit it
                    // checked out.
                    HealPublisherBoundStep {
                      step: heal_publisher_only_when_repair_owed(step: seed_lane.guard),
                      role: capability_neutral,
                    },
                    HealPublisherBoundStep {
                      step: heal_publisher_only_when_repair_owed(step: seed_lane.checkout),
                      role: capability_neutral,
                    },
                  ],
                  concat(
                    lane_bound,
                    [
                      HealPublisherBoundStep {
                        step: heal_publisher_only_when_repair_owed(step: build_step),
                        role: consumes_only(capabilities: [CargoCapability]),
                      },
                    ]
                  )
                )
              )
          }
      }
    }
  }
}

// THE REF EXPRESSIONS AND PRODUCER RECORDS THE WALL IS SHOWN, projected from the same source the steps
// were built from. The own-checkout route declares nothing and passes nothing, which is also why its
// emitted bytes are unchanged by this work: no seed, no verifier, no Needs ref to admit.
fn heal_publisher_declared_ref_expressions(source: PublisherCompilerSource) -> List<Expression> {
  match source {
    PublisherBuildsItsOwnCheckout { required_bins: _ } => []
    PublisherRunsUnderComposedSeed { seed: seed, overlay: _ } => seed_pin_verifier_declared_expressions(seed: seed)
  }
}

// THE VERIFIER JOB THE SEED ROUTE CANNOT RUN WITHOUT, or its absence for the route that needs none. One
// function answers both arms so the publisher cannot emit a seed checkout whose job was forgotten: the
// own-checkout route gets Absent and keeps `needs: []`, the seed route gets the job its checkout names.
fn heal_publisher_verifier_job(source: PublisherCompilerSource, job: HealPublisherPolicy) -> Outcome<Optional<Job>> {
  match source {
    PublisherBuildsItsOwnCheckout { required_bins: _ } =>
      outcome_accepted(value: Absent {  })
    PublisherRunsUnderComposedSeed { seed: seed, overlay: _ } =>
      match seed_source_pin(source: seed.source) {
        Absent {  } => outcome_rejected(
          d: module_unavailable_diagnostic(
            reason: seed_source_refusal_reason(source: seed.source),
            module_file: "gunbc.heal_publisher_workflow"
          )
        )
        Present { value: pin } =>
          bind_outcome(
            o: seed_pin_verifier_job(
              seed: seed,
              pin: pin,
              runner: ci_runner_target_spec(target: job.runner)
            ),
            f: fn(verifier) { outcome_accepted(value: Present { value: verifier }) }
          )
      }
  }
}

// THE STEP LIST THE JOB RUNDS, in one order, from values the entrypoint resolved: the repository checkout,
// the candidate download, the route's build steps, then the credential-free gate and the publication. The
// gate and the publication are built here rather than at the call site because their order
// against the build steps is part of the isolation story - the gate must run after the compiler exists and
// before anything can be minted.
fn heal_publisher_bound_steps(
  profile: RepositoryCiProfile,
  job: HealPublisherPolicy,
  route_steps: List<HealPublisherBoundStep>,
  source: PublisherCompilerSource
) -> Outcome<List<HealPublisherBoundStep>> {
  // THE GATE'S AND THE PUBLICATION'S OWN REFUSALS TRAVEL OUTWARD. Both builders say which statement of
  // which row they could not fold; folding their verdicts into a bare absence and letting a caller infer
  // a cause from the row would report a failure that did not happen.
  bind_outcome(
    o: heal_publisher_admit_step(profile: profile, job: job, source: source),
    f: fn(admit_step) {
      bind_outcome(
        o: heal_publisher_publish_step(profile: profile, job: job, source: source),
        f: fn(publish_step) {
          outcome_accepted(
            value: concat(
              concat(
                [
                  HealPublisherBoundStep {
                    step: heal_publisher_checkout_step(),
                    role: capability_neutral,
                  },
                  HealPublisherBoundStep {
                    step: heal_publisher_download_step(),
                    role: capability_neutral,
                  },
                ],
                route_steps
              ),
              [
                HealPublisherBoundStep { step: admit_step, role: capability_neutral },
                HealPublisherBoundStep { step: publish_step, role: capability_neutral },
              ]
            )
          )
        }
      )
    }
  )
}


fn heal_publisher_capability_closure_holds(steps: List<HealPublisherBoundStep>) -> Bool {
  capability_closure_is_closed(
    v: workflow_job_capability_closure(
      steps: list_map(
        xs: steps,
        f: fn(b) { CapabilityAnnotatedStep { step_name: step_display_name(s: b.step), role: b.role } },
      )
    ),
  )
}

// pull_requests: WRITE is the receipt comment, posted with the job's own token after the publication
// has landed and been read back; it carries no authority over the branch. (It was READ before this
// job answered for the PR-code producer's receipt.)
// actions: READ is the artifact download from another run. contents: READ is this checkout.
// pull_requests: READ is the subject join the admission makes. id-token: WRITE is the federation,
// and it is the only write on the job -- the publication credential itself is not a GitHub Actions
// grant at all but an App installation token minted inside the publish step.
// THE THREE PERMISSIONS THAT ARE THE EMITTER'S FLOOR stay here: contents READ is this checkout, actions
// READ is the artifact download from another run, and id-token WRITE is the federation - a job that cannot
// mint an identity token cannot publish at all, in any repository. pull_requests is the ROW's, because that
// is the one write whose need follows from how the repository publishes rather than from what the job runs
// (see HealPublisherPolicy.pull_requests).
fn heal_publisher_job_permissions(pull_requests: PermissionLevel) -> WorkflowPermissions {
  WorkflowPermissions {
    contents: Present { value: PermRead },
    pull_requests: Present { value: pull_requests },
    issues: none,
    actions: Present { value: PermRead },
    id_token: Present { value: PermWrite }
  }
}

// THE PUBLISHER JOB, BUILT FROM THE ROW THE EMITTER WAS GIVEN: its id, its machine class and its ceiling
// are reads of that one row, so a repository that declares no publisher has no publisher job -- not a job
// with a placeholder id downloading a candidate nothing sealed.
fn heal_publisher_job(
  profile: RepositoryCiProfile,
  job: HealPublisherPolicy,
  steps: List<HealPublisherBoundStep>,
  needs: List<String>
) -> Job {
  Job {
    id: job.job_id as String,
    name: none,
    // THE RUNNER IS A TARGET, PROJECTED. Stating the machine class here as a label was a third place that
    // had to know the publisher runs on a hosted runner - beside the profile row and the federation
    // condition that requires runner_environment == github-hosted. Selecting the target keeps the label,
    // the memory regime and the federation premise coming from one row.
    runner: ci_runner_target_spec(target: job.runner),
    environment: none,
    steps: list_map(xs: steps, f: fn(b) { b.step }),
    // The seed route's checkout names the seed-pin verifier's declared output, so the publisher waits on
    // the job that proves the pin; the own-checkout route needs nobody and says so with an empty list.
    needs: needs,
    env: none,
    outputs: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: heal_publisher_job_condition() },
    timeout_minutes: Present { value: minute_count(m: job.timeout) },
    continue_on_error: none,
    concurrency: none,
    permissions: Present { value: heal_publisher_job_permissions(pull_requests: job.pull_requests) }
  }
}

// THE CENSUS' VIEW OF A DECLARED PUBLISHER, for the one reader that enumerates a repository's own committed
// workflows rather than being handed a row: gunbc.runner.runner_fleet_job_timeout_census passes this
// repository's row explicitly, so no global is read on its behalf. A row that refuses yields NO workflow
// rather than a guessed one, and the census' own claim that every committed artifact appears is what makes
// that absence loud.
fn heal_publisher_census_workflow(profile: RepositoryCiProfile) -> Optional<Workflow> {
  // A REFUSED ROW YIELDS NO CENSUS ROW, under the SAME ADMISSION the emission applies. This is not the
  // place a refusal's diagnostics surface: a census is a projection over committed artifacts, and the
  // claim `gunbc.runner.runner_fleet_job_timeout_census`'s control
  // `the_census_domain_is_explained_and_reads_the_authorities` is what turns a committed artifact missing
  // from it red. The verdict therefore collapses to "no row" here; the emission entrypoint is where a
  // caller gets the reason to read, with the diagnostics that refused still attached to it.
  match repository_ci_profile_refusal(profile: profile) {
    Present { value: _ } => Absent
    Absent =>
      match profile.publisher {
        Absent => Absent
        Present { value: job } =>
          // THE SAME RESOLUTION THE RENDER USES, not a second reading of the row: one function resolves
          // the compiler source, the route and the step list, and both consumers ask it. A row the
          // publisher cannot be built from -- a `CompilerSource` with no publisher consumer -- yields no
          // census row here and is refused with its reason by the render entrypoint.
          match profile_publisher_compiler_source(profile: profile) {
            Absent => Absent
            Present { value: source } =>
              match heal_publisher_resolved_steps(profile: profile, job: job, source: source) {
                Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => Absent
                Accepted { value: steps, diagnostics: _ } =>
                  match heal_publisher_verifier_job(source: source, job: job) {
                    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => Absent {  }
                    Accepted { value: verifier_job, diagnostics: _ } =>
                      Present { value: heal_publisher_workflow_of(profile: profile, job: job, steps: steps, verifier_job: verifier_job) }
                  }
              }
          }
      }
  }
}

fn heal_publisher_workflow_of(
  profile: RepositoryCiProfile,
  job: HealPublisherPolicy,
  steps: List<HealPublisherBoundStep>,
  verifier_job: Optional<Job>
) -> Workflow {
  Workflow {
  name: job.workflow_name as String,
  run_name: none,
  on: [
    WorkflowRunTriggered {
      // THE WORKFLOW THAT SEALS THE CANDIDATE THIS JOB PUBLISHES, named by the profile rather than
      // imported from the heal emitter: workflow_run fires on a NAME, and a name the profile does not
      // declare is a publisher that never runs. The profile admits that pairing
      // (gunbc.ci.repository_ci_profile profile_publisher_trigger_is_declared), so the trigger and the
      // sealing job cannot drift apart silently.
      workflows: [job.trigger_workflow as String],
      branches: heal_publisher_triggering_branches,
      activity: WorkflowRunCompletedActivity
    }
  ],
  concurrency: Present { value: heal_publisher_concurrency },
  env: Present {
    value: [
      kv(key: "CARGO_TERM_COLOR", value: yaml_string(s: "always"))
    ]
  },
  permissions: none,
  jobs: concat(
    match verifier_job {
      Present { value: v } => [v]
      Absent {  } => []
    },
    [
      heal_publisher_job(
        profile: profile,
        job: job,
        steps: steps,
        needs: match verifier_job {
          Present { value: v } => [v.id]
          Absent {  } => []
        }
      )
    ]
  )
}
}

// ── The isolation invariant, asked of both emitted jobs ──────────────────────────────────────

fn step_input_names(step: Step) -> List<String> {
  match step {
    RunStep { name: _, id: _, run: _, shell: _, env: _, working_directory: _, if_condition: _, continue_on_error: _, timeout_minutes: _ } => []
    UsesStep { name: _, id: _, uses: _, with: w, env: _, if_condition: _, continue_on_error: _, timeout_minutes: _ } =>
      match w {
        Absent => []
        Present { value: rows } => map(rows, row => row.key as String)
      }
  }
}

// A CHECKOUT THAT NAMES A REF OR A REPOSITORY IS FETCHING SOMETHING OTHER THAN THIS WORKFLOW'S OWN
// COMMIT, and for this job the only thing it could name is the branch under review. That is the
// second isolation direction: the phase holding publication authority must not obtain the candidate's
// code, and the check reads the emitted steps rather than trusting the author.
fn checkout_step_names_another_subject(step: Step) -> Bool {
  step_uses_action(step: step, action: checkout_action)
    && any(step_input_names(step: step), k => k == "ref" || k == "repository")
}

// THE ONE REFERENCE A PUBLISHER MAY CHECK OUT BESIDES ITS OWN COMMIT is the public seed's pinned commit,
// named as the output of the job that proves the pin exists. That checkout is the compiler this repository
// exists to run under - pinned by the row, not by the event, and not the candidate's code - so naming it is
// not the boundary violation. A checkout of any other repository, or of a ref that is not the verifier's
// declared output, still is: this comparison used to be against `seed.ref_name`, a string that could name a
// branch, which is the thing the exact-pin work removed from the model.
fn publisher_input_names_undeclared_subject(input: YamlKeyValue, seed: Optional<PublicSeed>) -> Bool {
  match seed {
    Absent => true
    Present { value: s } =>
      match input.value {
        YamlString { value: v } =>
          if input.key == "repository" {
            v != s.repository as String
          } else if input.key == "ref" {
            match seed_pin_checkout_ref_expression(seed: s) {
              Present { value: e } => v != interpolate(expression: e)
              Absent {  } => true
            }
          } else {
            true
          }
        _ => true
      }
  }
}

fn publisher_checkout_fetches_candidate_code(step: Step, seed: Optional<PublicSeed>) -> Bool {
  if step_uses_action(step: step, action: checkout_action) == false {
    false
  } else {
    match step {
      UsesStep {
        name: _, id: _, uses: _, with: w, env: _, if_condition: _, continue_on_error: _, timeout_minutes: _
      } =>
        match w {
          Absent => false
          Present { value: rows } =>
            any(
              rows,
              row =>
                (row.key == "ref" || row.key == "repository")
                  && publisher_input_names_undeclared_subject(input: row, seed: seed)
            )
        }
      _ => false
    }
  }
}

fn publisher_job_executes_candidate_code(steps: List<HealPublisherBoundStep>, profile: RepositoryCiProfile) -> Bool {
  any(
    steps,
    b => publisher_checkout_fetches_candidate_code(step: b.step, seed: profile_public_seed(profile: profile))
  )
}

// The candidate side of the boundary is the heal row OF THE ADMITTED PROFILE, read by matching: a
// repository with no heal job has no candidate, and the boundary is then vacuous rather than assumed safe.
// It took the profile rather than the job because the candidate is the OTHER job in the same row - reading
// gunbc's global heal row here would let the publisher admit one repository and boundary-check another.
fn publication_candidate_holds_publication_credential(profile: RepositoryCiProfile) -> Bool {
  match profile.heal {
    Absent => false
    Present { value: policy } => heal_job_holds_publication_credential(job: policy)
  }
}

fn heal_publication_phases_isolated(steps: List<HealPublisherBoundStep>, profile: RepositoryCiProfile) -> Bool {
  phases_are_isolated(s: phase_isolation(
    candidate_holds_publication_credential: publication_candidate_holds_publication_credential(profile: profile),
    candidate_credential_detail: "the heal candidate job carries a write grant or federates to a cloud identity",
    publication_executes_candidate_code: publisher_job_executes_candidate_code(steps: steps, profile: profile),
    publication_execution_detail: "the publisher job checks out a ref or repository other than its own default-branch commit"
  ))
}

type HealPublisherWorkflowGenerationOutcome
  = HealPublisherWorkflowGenerated { content: String }
  | HealPublisherWorkflowGenerationRefused { reason: String }

data heal_publisher_isolation_refusal_reason: String = "REFUSED gunbc.heal_publisher_workflow: the two phases are not isolated -- either the candidate job holds a publication credential or the publisher job obtains the candidate's code. No workflow yaml was emitted; this is the invariant gunbc.heal_publication_boundary states and gunbc#10887 violated."

data heal_publisher_capability_refusal_reason: String = "REFUSED gunbc.heal_publisher_workflow: the job's capability closure does not hold. No workflow yaml was emitted."

// WHAT THE ROW FORBIDS BEFORE THE ADMISSIONS RUN: a publisher job for a repository that declares no
// publisher is a job that downloads a candidate nothing seals.


// THE PUBLISHER EMITTER, REFUSING BEFORE IT RENDERS. `gunbc#10887`'s shape -- a heal-publish.yml with no
// heal.yml behind it, a job that fires with nothing to publish -- was reachable here because the emitter
// could still build a publisher job when the profile declared none: the placeholder row kept the id, the
// runner and the trigger rendering happily. Now presence is a match, the placeholder rows are deleted, and
// a repository with no publisher gets a refusal that says what to delete or what to declare.
fn expected_heal_publisher_workflow_yml() -> HealPublisherWorkflowGenerationOutcome {
  heal_publisher_workflow_yml_for(profile: gunbc_repository_ci_profile())
}

fn heal_publisher_workflow_yml_for(profile: RepositoryCiProfile) -> HealPublisherWorkflowGenerationOutcome {
  // THE ROW IT WAS HANDED IS ADMITTED BEFORE A BYTE IS RENDERED, and it is the SAME row the builders read:
  // the publisher's job, its trigger check, its candidate artifact and the heal row the publication boundary
  // inspects all come from this one profile, so an emitter that admitted one repository and rendered another
  // could not be built. One match, on the parameter, at the entrypoint.
  match repository_ci_profile_refusal(profile: profile) {
    Present { value: reason } => HealPublisherWorkflowGenerationRefused { reason: reason as String }
    Absent => heal_publisher_workflow_yml_of_admitted_row(profile: profile)
  }
}

// THE ROUTE AND THE COMPOSITION, resolved once from the row. Every consumer - the render below and the
// census in gunbc.runner.runner_fleet_job_timeout_census - asks the SAME resolution, so a check can never be
// asked of a step list other than the one that gets rendered.
fn heal_publisher_resolved_steps(
  profile: RepositoryCiProfile,
  job: HealPublisherPolicy,
  source: PublisherCompilerSource
) -> Outcome<List<HealPublisherBoundStep>> {
  bind_outcome(
    o: heal_publisher_route_steps(source: source),
    f: fn(route_steps) {
      heal_publisher_bound_steps(
        profile: profile,
        job: job,
        route_steps: route_steps,
        source: source
      )
    }
  )
}

// THE REFUSAL NAMES WHAT ACTUALLY REFUSED. The step builders carry their own diagnostics - the seed
// grammar says which statement of which seed row it could not fold, the phase script says which subject it
// could not render - and the reason a caller reads is composed from those lexemes. Nothing here re-derives
// a cause from the row: a message that guessed would report a failure that did not happen and send an
// operator to a row that was never the problem.
fn heal_publisher_steps_refusal_reason(diagnostics: NonEmptyDiagnostics) -> String {
  concat(
    "REFUSE gunbc.heal_publisher_workflow: the publisher's steps were refused under ",
    concat(
      diagnostics_reason_lexemes(diagnostics: diagnostics),
      ". No workflow is emitted rather than one whose build or phases the model cannot fold; the lexeme names the declaration to fix."
    )
  )
}

// THE RENDER AND EVERY CHECK ARE ASKED OF THE SAME STEP LIST: the job's steps are resolved once, and the
// checkout admission, the toolchain-home standing, the isolation invariant, the capability closure and the
// render all receive THAT list. A check asked of a different list than the one rendered is the failure this
// module has been caught at three times, each time by a global read surviving inside a row-parameterised
// path.
fn heal_publisher_workflow_yml_of_admitted_row(profile: RepositoryCiProfile) -> HealPublisherWorkflowGenerationOutcome {
  match profile.publisher {
    Absent => HealPublisherWorkflowGenerationRefused { reason: heal_publisher_no_job_refusal_reason }
    Present { value: job } =>
      // THE COMPILER SOURCE IS RESOLVED BEFORE A SINGLE STEP IS BUILT. The sum the row carries has a case
      // this emitter has no consumer for, and the honest response to being asked for it is this refusal --
      // not a step list assembled from the other case's recipe, which is what happened while the phases
      // dispatched on the presence of a composition instead of on the source that named the compiler.
      match profile_publisher_compiler_source(profile: profile) {
        Absent => HealPublisherWorkflowGenerationRefused { reason: heal_publisher_seed_route_without_a_composition_refusal_reason }
        Present { value: source } =>
      match heal_publisher_resolved_steps(profile: profile, job: job, source: source) {
        Rejected { diagnostics: d } => HealPublisherWorkflowGenerationRefused { reason: heal_publisher_steps_refusal_reason(diagnostics: d) }
        Accepted { value: steps, diagnostics: _ } =>
      match heal_publisher_verifier_job(source: source, job: job) {
        Rejected { diagnostics: d } => HealPublisherWorkflowGenerationRefused { reason: heal_publisher_steps_refusal_reason(diagnostics: d) }
        Accepted { value: verifier_job, diagnostics: _ } =>
      let publisher_workflow = heal_publisher_workflow_of(profile: profile, job: job, steps: steps, verifier_job: verifier_job)
      match admit_workflow_checkout_refs(
        workflow: publisher_workflow,
        declared: heal_publisher_declared_ref_expressions(source: source)
      ) {
    WorkflowCheckoutRefsRefused { refusals: crs } =>
      HealPublisherWorkflowGenerationRefused { reason: workflow_checkout_refs_refusal_reason(workflow_module: "gunbc.heal_publisher_workflow", refusals: crs) }
    WorkflowCheckoutRefsAdmitted =>
      match admit_workflow_toolchain_homes(workflow: publisher_workflow) {
    WorkflowToolchainHomeRefused { job_id: j, refusal: r } =>
      HealPublisherWorkflowGenerationRefused { reason: toolchain_home_refusal_reason(job_id: j, refusal: r) }
    WorkflowToolchainHomesAdmitted =>
      if !heal_publication_phases_isolated(steps: steps, profile: profile) {
        HealPublisherWorkflowGenerationRefused { reason: heal_publisher_isolation_refusal_reason }
      } else if !heal_publisher_capability_closure_holds(steps: steps) {
        HealPublisherWorkflowGenerationRefused { reason: heal_publisher_capability_refusal_reason }
      } else {
        match emit_yaml(v: project_workflow_to_yaml(workflow: publisher_workflow)) {
          EmittedYaml { text: t } => HealPublisherWorkflowGenerated { content: t }
          YamlEmitRefused { path: p, reason: r } => HealPublisherWorkflowGenerationRefused { reason: yaml_emit_refusal_text(module_path: "gunbc.heal_publisher_workflow", path: p, reason: r) }
        }
      }
      }
  }
  }
  }
  }
  }
}
